Ed Kolis wrote:

One of the advantages of NetHack is it has playing skill issues that go
on and on until you can consistently ascend several characters in a row.
Until you've ascended several characters you may not be sure what lesson
you'll end up learning next.  Your questions suggest you are now working
your way through the mid-game of levels before Medusa.


> Why is it necessary to manually #enhance one's weapon skills? Why can't
> the skills automatically level up?

Because skill slots are a finite resource that requires management and a
strategy for how and when to spend them.  If slots automatically level
up you end up wasting some on skills you should never have advanced at
the cost of problems later in the game.

> Also, is it just me or does the whole weapon-skill system just serve
> the purpose of homogenizing weapon use for each character class?

Among characters of the same class, until you learn more about the skill
slot system it can seem that way.  Once you've learned that it gives a
range of options within a framework.  Sort of like using a muscial scale
to play a short tune - There are many of them possible and some sound
pretty bad while others sound pretty good.

> That is, if I start as a Barbarian with a battle-axe, there is no
> incentive for me to swap to some other weapon (e.g. two-handed sword),
> even if the other weapon is superior,

There is a set of issues you have not encountered yet that give those
options meaning.

> because if I do, I'll be missing
> 90% of the time, and it will take forever to gain any skill in
> two-handed sword to make it worth wielding!

Same quote, but the set of issues is a different one.

> So that makes most of the
> weapons found in the dungeon absolutely useless to any character!

In fact that's true.  Weapons abound so no player who wants weapons
ever lacks for them.  But there are far more crappy weapons than good
ones.  Like skill slots being a limited resource so you need to learn
the options, the good quality weapons are a limited resource so you need
to learn the options.

Using what crappy weapons happen to be on hand is often a survival
tactic.  Focusing on the good weapons and good skill schools is a need
to be able to continue deeper into the dungeon.  Those two are sometimes
in conflict and sometimes in alignment.  That is yet another reason why
NetHack stays interesting year after year.


